        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x^2+x+1)(x^2+5x+1)$.\n\nUse parts (a) and (b), then factor as a quadratic in $x^2+1$.",
          "worked": "Using parts (a) and (b),\n$$\\begin{align*}x^4+6x^3+7x^2+6x+1&=(x^2+1)^2-2x^2+6x(x^2+1)+7x^2\\\\x^4+6x^3+7x^2+6x+1&=(x^2+1)^2+6x(x^2+1)+5x^2.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $u=x^2+1$. Since $1+5=6$ and $1\\cdot5=5$,\n$$\\begin{align*}u^2+6xu+5x^2&=(u+x)(u+5x).\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting back gives\n$$x^4+6x^3+7x^2+6x+1=(x^2+x+1)(x^2+5x+1).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
